    "agent_last_message": "The tool is implemented in `output/` and builds cleanly with `cargo build --release --manifest-path output/Cargo.toml`. I ran it against the spec's edge cases and the output matched each one.\n\n**Files**\n- `output/Cargo.toml`: package manifest with an empty `[dependencies]` table.\n- `output/src/main.rs`: the whole implementation, standard library only.\n\n**Checks run**\n- `hello` (no newline): 1 line, 1 word, 5 characters.\n- `hello\\n`: 1 line, 1 word, 6 characters.\n- Empty file: all zeros and `top_words: []`.\n- Whitespace-only file (including `\\v` and `\\f`): 0 words, lines and characters counted.\n- Mixed-case and punctuated words: `The`/`the`/`THE` merge to `the`, while `cat,` and `cat.` stay separate. Quotes and backslashes in words are escaped in the JSON.\n- Exit codes: a missing input file, a missing `--output`, an unknown flag, and a flag with no value all exit 1. A successful run exits 0.\n\n**Choices I made where the spec was silent**\n- **Invalid UTF-8 input exits 1** as \"malformed\". The spec doesn't define malformed, so this is my reading. Tell me if you'd rather have byte-oriented processing that never rejects a file.\n- **Output write failures exit 1**, treating an unwritable `--output` path as an invalid invocation.\n- **Panics exit 2**, via `catch_unwind`. An out-of-memory abort cannot be caught in Rust, so it will not produce exit 2.\n- **`--flag=value` form** is accepted alongside `--flag value`. `-h`/`--help` prints usage and exits 0.\n- **Lowercasing** uses Unicode lowercase for non-ASCII words. Words are still split only on the spec's six ASCII whitespace characters.\n\nCargo isn't on the default PATH in this shell, so I ran it from `/usr/local/rustup/toolchains/stable-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/bin`. The project itself has no dependency on that path. The directory is not a git repo, so I made no commit.",
